You're on the right track with your approach, but there's a small issue with how the **nightmode layer** is being applied. The current setup uses a `label` with a `:before` pseudo-element that becomes visible when the checkbox is checked. However, this `label` is not a full overlay—it's just a pseudo-element that's positioned absolutely, and it doesn't cover the entire screen or prevent interaction with the content behind it.

To make the **nightmode layer** cover the entire screen and prevent interaction with the content behind it (like selecting text), you need to use a **full-screen overlay** that is **positioned absolutely or fixed** and has a `z-index` higher than the rest of the content.

---

### ✅ Solution: Use a Full-Screen Overlay

Here's how you can modify your HTML and CSS to achieve this:

---

### ✅ Updated HTML (Add a new `div` for the nightmode overlay)

```html
<div class="switch-container">
  <input type="checkbox" id="switch">
  <label for="switch">Energy saving nightmode layer</label>
</div>

<!-- Add this new div for the nightmode overlay -->
<div class="nightmode-overlay" id="nightmode-overlay">
  <p>Overlay content (optional)</p>
</div>
```

---

### ✅ Updated CSS (Add the overlay styles)

```css
/* Add this to your CSS */
.nightmode-overlay {
  position: fixed;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.7); /* Dark overlay */
  z-index: 1000; /* Make sure it's above everything */
  display: none; /* Hidden by default */
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
  color: white;
  font-size: 24px;
  text-align: center;
}

/* Show the overlay when the checkbox is checked */
#switch:checked ~ .nightmode-overlay {
  display: flex;
}
```

